  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.707pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(191.45pt, 236.78pt, " 49")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.302pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(200.21pt, 240.26pt, " and by Prassl et al. using lipoproteins for fast variations.")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.707pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(447.46pt, 236.78pt, "50")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.302pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 252.5pt, "Photoexcitation measurements on HbCO were conducted using linearly polarized light, which allowed ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 264.74pt, "the observation of photobleaching. In contrast, Cammarata et al. used circularly polarized light inducing ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 276.86pt, "structural changes in the protein due to photolysis. These preliminary SAXS experiments demonstrate ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 289.13pt, "that the described microfluidic device can be used for photoexcitation and T-jump studies on proteins, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 301.37pt, "as well as on other photosensitive samples.  ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 331.49pt, "Conclusions ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 352.25pt, "A microfluidic device which is transparent to visible an UV light down to 340 nm on one direction, and ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 364.37pt, "transparent to X-rays in the perpendicular direction, has been described. The fabrication process combines ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 376.61pt, "standard UV lithography with a lamination process using SUEX dry resist film and IM-PMMA. It does not ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 388.85pt, "necessarily require a clean room environment, and it can be easily downscaled to microchannel dimensions of ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 400.97pt, "tens of microns (limited by the obtainable resolution with the dry film resist). The device has been successfully ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 413.21pt, "employed for photoexcitation experiments using either UV-Vis spectroscopy or SAXS to monitor light-induced ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 425.45pt, "changes. The small dimensions of the channel ensure that the entire sample volume is illuminated by light over ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 437.69pt, "the range of different wavelengths investigated herein (365 nm, 450 nm, 513 nm). SAXS results obtained from ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 449.81pt, "proteins measured in the microchannel show that the scattering signal allows to detect minute differences, like ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 462.05pt, "for Hb and HbCO, fully exploiting the small sample consumption due to microfluidics. Generally, ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.302pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(476.98pt, 462.05pt, "the device ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 474.31pt, "dimensions can be tailored both on the adsorption length of the photoexcitation light source, and on ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 486.55pt, "the size and penetration depth of the measuring X-ray beam, ensuring efficient photoexcitation in ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 498.67pt, "optimized conditions for SAXS experiments. ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(268.37pt, 498.67pt, " Furthermore, the device can be easily cleaned and reused, as ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 510.91pt, "its materials are compatible with standard cleaning solutions commonly used for proteins and lipids. The device ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 523.15pt, "provides a versatile platform for photoexcitation studies and laser induced thermal jump (T-jump) experiments.  ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 535.27pt, "While its performance has been demonstrated on protein samples, the same concept can be readily extended ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 547.51pt, "to inorganic or hybrid systems where simultaneous optical excitation and X-ray probing (pump-probe) are ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 559.75pt, "required. More complex geometries can also be obtained, for example inserting mixing regions before the ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 571.99pt, "observation channel. Moreover, the device could be used in a ´light-reconfigurable´ way")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 6.48pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(445.78pt, 568.51pt, "27")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(452.26pt, 571.99pt, " where injection, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 584.11pt, "pumping, mixing, and sorting are controlled by light stimulation in one direction, while structural ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 596.23pt, "characterization is simultaneously performed in the perpendicular direction using X-rays.  ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 634.51pt, "Author contributions ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 651.19pt, "BM conceptualization, investigation, device fabrication, formal analysis, draft writing. S.K. conceptualization, ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 663.46pt, "investigation, formal analysis. B.S investigation, formal analysis. A.T and M.R device fabrication. A.H. ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 675.7pt, "Conceptualization, SAXS investigation, formal analysis, fund raising. All authors contributed to review and editing ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 687.94pt, "of the manuscript. ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 722.02pt, "Acknowledgements ")
  #set text(font: "Calibri", size: 10.302pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72.024pt, 738.7pt, "The authors gratefully thank A. Radeticchio and C. Morello for technical assistance, and H. Annemarie ")
  #line-at(72.024pt, 750.936pt, "Reichl and M. Rödl for providing the fluoro-azobenzene compounds. ")
]

